WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:26.940
<v Ira Glass>So LuAnne Johnson was a teacher, and she wrote a book about her job, and she sold that book to Hollywood. And before long, her book was made into a movie called Dangerous Minds. Now it's also a TV show, also called Dangerous Minds. And now, every week, LuAnne Johnson can turn on the TV and see a teacher with her same name march around and do things she thinks are unethical, inadvisable, and sometimes just plain silly, like holding a school fundraiser in a strip club.
<v Ira Glass>卢安·约翰逊曾是一名教师，她写了一本关于她工作经历的书，并将其卖给了好莱坞。没过多久，她的书就被改编成了一部名为《危险游戏》的电影。现在，同名的电视剧也播出了。如今，卢安·约翰逊每周都能打开电视，看到一个与她同名的老师在电视里走来走去，做着她认为不道德、不明智，有时甚至非常荒谬的事情，比如在脱衣舞俱乐部举办学校筹款活动。

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:46.180
<v Luanne Johnson>Number one, there's no teacher I know who would do such a thing. It's illegal. I think you'd lose your job, and I would hope you would lose your job. But I just objected to that. And I would never do it.
<v Luanne Johnson>第一，我认识的老师里没有人会做这种事。这是非法的。我想你会丢掉工作，我也希望你会丢掉工作。但我只是对此表示反对。我永远不会这么做。

00:00:46.180 --> 00:01:24.820
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, deals with the devil. As we do every week, we've invited a variety of writers and performers onto the show with their stories. This week, all stories about selling your soul, making Faustian bargains. In our program today, Act One, Dangerous Minds. Act Two-- Act Two is actually a found tape of Satan's own telemarketer. Act Three, Voyager. Act Four, Carmen Becomes Faust. Act Five, First Contact. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎收听来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，我是主持人艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目主题是与魔鬼做交易。和每周一样，我们邀请了各种作家和表演者来分享他们的故事。本周的所有故事都与出卖灵魂、进行浮士德式的交易有关。今天的节目内容包括：第一幕，《危险游戏》。第二幕——第二幕其实是一段撒旦专属电话推销员的录音。第三幕，《航海家》。第四幕，《卡门变身浮士德》。第五幕，《第一次接触》。请继续收听。

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:43.950
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Dangerous Minds. First, a word about LuAnne Johnson's book, the one that they adapted very freely into the movie and the TV show Dangerous Minds. It was called My Posse Don't Do Homework. LuAnne Johnson taught in the public schools, and she says that contrary to what you may see on the evening news, it was not a war zone.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，《危险游戏》。首先，聊聊卢安·约翰逊的书，那本被改编成电影和电视剧《危险游戏》的原著。书名叫做《我的学生不做作业》。卢安·约翰逊曾在公立学校任教，她说，与你在晚间新闻中看到的相反，学校并非战区。

00:01:43.950 --> 00:02:11.020
<v Luanne Johnson>No, that's one of the reasons I wrote that book, is I felt like people were just writing off kids. You know, 13, 14 years old, saying, well, they're hopeless, they're useless, and there's nothing we can do. All the things I was reading were about how horrible they are, and they have no respect for people, and they don't care, and they're unteachable. And I just don't feel that's true. I think that's really wrong.
<v Luanne Johnson>不，这就是我写那本书的原因之一，我觉得人们只是在否定这些孩子。你知道，那些13、14岁的孩子，人们说，嗯，他们没救了，他们没用了，我们什么也做不了。我读到的所有东西都是关于他们有多可怕，他们不尊重人，他们不在乎，他们无法教导。我只是觉得那不是真的。我认为那大错特错。

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:23.370
<v Ira Glass>Well, LuAnne Johnson's deal with the devil proceeded in several stages. First the film, which starred Michelle Pfeiffer, came out, I don't know, two years ago I think, three years ago. And LuAnne Johnson did like some things about the film.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，卢安·约翰逊与魔鬼的交易经历了几个阶段。首先是电影，由米歇尔·菲佛主演，我不记得了，大概是两三年前上映的吧。卢安·约翰逊确实喜欢电影中的某些东西。

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:43.420
<v Luanne Johnson>The primary lesson I always try to teach my students is you choose who you are. You can throw up your hands and blame everyone in the world and say, I can't earn an A, I can't do this, I have to do that. You don't have to do anything. You don't have to come to school. You choose to be here. Now you can choose to learn, or you can choose to vegetate. I think that came across in the movie, that there is hope, that you're not a victim.
<v Luanne Johnson>我一直试图教给学生的首要课程是，你选择成为什么样的人。你可以摊开双手抱怨全世界，说我拿不到A，我做不到这个，我必须做那个。你其实什么都不必做。你不必来学校。是你选择来这里的。现在你可以选择学习，也可以选择虚度光阴。我认为电影里传达出了这一点，即希望是存在的，你不是受害者。

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:48.250
<v Student 1>Man, you don't understand nothing. I mean, you don't come from where we live. You're not bussed here.
<v Student 1>伙计，你根本什么都不懂。我是说，你不是来自我们住的地方。你不是坐校车来的。

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.600
<v Michelle Pfeiffer>Do you have a choice to get on that bus?
<v Michelle Pfeiffer>你有选择不坐那辆车的权利吗？

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.260
<v Student 1>Man, you come and live in my neighborhood for one week, and then you tell me if you've got a choice.
<v Student 1>伙计，你来我们社区住上一周，然后再告诉我你有没有选择。

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:17.070
<v Michelle Pfeiffer>There are a lot of people who live in your neighborhood who choose not to get on that bus. The people who choose to get on that bus, which are you, are the people who are saying I will not carry myself down to die. When I go to my grave, my head will be high. That is a choice. There are no victims in this classroom.
<v Michelle Pfeiffer>在你那个社区里，有很多人选择不坐那辆校车。而选择坐校车的人，也就是你们，是在说我不会自甘堕落。当我走进坟墓时，我会昂首挺胸。那是一种选择。在这个教室里没有受害者。

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:54.700
<v Ira Glass>But the movie took some liberties with LuAnne Johnson's book. For instance, the filmmakers made her mixed race class into an all minority class. The filmmakers invented fictional black parents who did not want their kids to learn. The filmmakers organized the film around two stories which did not happen in real life. In one, LuAnne Johnson gets so overwhelmed by how impossible it is to make progress with her kids that after just a few months, she decides to quit. And in the other, a student is shot and killed. And she feels that these things reinforce the very stereotypes that she was trying to dispel by writing her book in the first place.
<v Ira Glass>但电影对卢安·约翰逊的书进行了一些艺术加工。例如，电影制作人把她班上的混合种族学生改成了全少数族裔班级。制作人虚构了一些不想让孩子学习的黑人父母。制作人围绕两个现实中并未发生的故事来组织电影。在一个故事里，卢安·约翰逊对教育这些孩子的无力感感到崩溃，仅仅几个月后就决定辞职。在另一个故事里，一名学生被枪杀。她认为这些内容强化了她写书初衷正是为了消除的刻板印象。

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:04.860
<v Luanne Johnson>And I think the movie really promotes racial tension by constantly pitting black against white, having the kids call me "white bread," which never happened. It just, it didn't happen.
<v Luanne Johnson>我认为这部电影通过不断挑起黑人与白人的对立来煽动种族紧张关系，让孩子们叫我“白面包”，这从未发生过。它就是，它没有发生过。

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:27.760
<v Luanne Johnson>And I said to them, why'd they do that? And they said, well, we found it hard to believe that a classroom of black and Hispanic kids would welcome a white teacher and give her any respect. And I said, but it's not a matter of your skin color, it's a matter of how you treat them. But they couldn't believe that. And they still don't. And I think there are a lot of people who do believe that you can't walk into a classroom filled with minority kids and they're going to treat you right.
<v Luanne Johnson>我对他们说，你们为什么要那样做？他们说，嗯，我们觉得很难相信一个由黑人和西班牙裔孩子组成的班级会欢迎一位白人老师并给予她任何尊重。我说，但这与你们的肤色无关，这取决于你们如何对待他们。但他们无法相信。他们现在仍然不相信。我认为有很多人确实认为，你不可能走进一个满是少数族裔孩子的教室，而他们会善待你。

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:36.110
<v Ira Glass>Around this time, her deal with, well, with Hollywood, let's just say, with Hollywood took other turns. A new printing of the book My Posse Don't Do Homework came out.
<v Ira Glass>就在这个时候，她与，嗯，与好莱坞的交易，我们就说与好莱坞的交易吧，发生了其他转折。《我的学生不做作业》出了新版。

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:39.930
<v Luanne Johnson>And when I got that in the mail, I opened it up. And it said Dangerous Minds.
<v Luanne Johnson>当我收到邮件打开它时，上面写着《危险游戏》。

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.620
<v Ira Glass>As the title it said Dangerous Minds?
<v Ira Glass>书名写的是《危险游戏》？

00:04:41.620 --> 00:05:04.870
<v Luanne Johnson>Yeah. It said Dangerous Minds, and it had the movie poster on it. And it said by LuAnne Johnson. And I thought, well, I didn't write a book called Dangerous Minds. How can they do this? So I called my editor, and I said, "How can you do this? How can you change the title of my book without even telling me?" And she said, "It's not your book. It's our book. You wrote it. We bought it. We own it."
<v Luanne Johnson>是的。上面写着《危险游戏》，还有电影海报。作者写的是卢安·约翰逊。我心想，嗯，我没写过一本叫《危险游戏》的书。他们怎么能这么做？所以我给编辑打了电话，我说：“你们怎么能这样？你们怎么能在不告诉我的情况下更改我的书名？”她说：“这不是你的书。这是我们的书。你写的。我们买了。它是我们的。”

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:14.010
<v Ira Glass>Not long after that, executives, executives somewhere in America, decided to exercise a clause in an old contract that she had signed, and her deal took this turn.
<v Ira Glass>没过多久，美国某处的执行官们决定行使她签署的一份旧合同中的条款，她的交易就这样发生了转变。

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:33.860
<v Luanne Johnson>Someone called me and said, did you hear? I heard they're making a TV show out of your book. And I said, oh  really? Oh, that's interesting. So I called my agent in Los Angeles and said, "They're making a TV series? I would like to consult, because I don't want them to do what they did with the movie, get so far removed from reality. I'd really like to have something to do this time."
<v Luanne Johnson>有人给我打电话问，你听说了吗？我听说他们要把你的书拍成电视剧。我说，哦真的吗？哦，那真有趣。所以我给在洛杉矶的经纪人打电话说：“他们要拍电视剧？我想参与咨询，因为我不希望他们像对待电影那样，离现实太远。这次我真的想做点什么。”

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:44.700
<v Luanne Johnson>So she called back later and said, "No, they said they don't need you. They don't want you. They can't afford you." And I said, "What do you mean? I know I'm a lot cheaper than those Hollywood people." So, call them back.
<v Luanne Johnson>后来她回电说：“不，他们说他们不需要你。他们不想要你。他们雇不起你。”我说：“你什么意思？我知道我比那些好莱坞的人便宜得多。”所以，回电给他们。

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.240
<v Ira Glass>That's right. Apparently they haven't heard about educational salaries in America.
<v Ira Glass>没错。显然他们还没听说过美国的教育工资水平。

00:05:49.240 --> 00:06:01.320
<v Luanne Johnson>Right. And then there was some discussion between her and them and me. And in fact, I asked one of the producers how much they're paying the writers. I said, "I heard you were paying them $50,000 each per episode." And he said, "Oh no, we're only paying them $30,000 plus perks."
<v Luanne Johnson>对。然后她和他们以及我之间进行了一些讨论。事实上，我问过其中一位制片人他们给编剧付多少钱。我说：“我听说你们每集给他们付5万美元。”他说：“哦不，我们只付给他们3万美元外加福利。”

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:01.940
<v Ira Glass>Per episode?
<v Ira Glass>每集？

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:17.640
<v Luanne Johnson>A week. And we asked them to pay $25,000 for a year. And they said, "Well, we'll see. Probably not."
<v Luanne Johnson>每周。而我们要求他们每年支付2.5万美元。他们说：“嗯，我们看看。大概不行。”

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:35.440
<v Ira Glass>Again, there are things about the TV series that LuAnne Johnson likes. She likes, especially, the feisty, in-your-face way that Annie Potts plays her, for example. But she also finds plenty that makes her uncomfortable. Like that episode where the students stage the fundraiser in the strip club. They hold something called a hair war.
<v Ira Glass>同样，电视剧里也有一些卢安·约翰逊喜欢的地方。例如，她特别喜欢安妮·波茨饰演她时那种活泼、直率的方式。但她也发现了许多让她不舒服的地方。比如那集学生在脱衣舞俱乐部举办筹款活动。他们举办了一场所谓的“发型战争”。

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.810
<v Annie Potts>What? What is a hair war?
<v Annie Potts>什么？什么是发型战争？

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.430
<v Student 2>It's kind of like a fashion show, only with hairdos.
<v Student 2>有点像时装秀，只不过主角是发型。

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:47.150
<v Annie Potts>You're trying to tell me that people will come and lay down good money to watch us get our hair curled?
<v Annie Potts>你是想告诉我人们会花大价钱来看我们卷头发吗？

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:51.080
<v Student 3>Where've you been, in a coma? This ain't your everyday, walk-around hair.
<v Student 3>你去哪了，在昏迷吗？这可不是你们平时随便弄的那种头发。

00:06:51.080 --> 00:07:08.760
<v Luanne Johnson>So I called the producer, Andy Schneider, and I said, "Andy, I'm very upset about this." He said, "Well, what's your problem?" And I said, "Where do I start? Would you like if your 14-year-old or 15-year-old daughter came home from school and you found out her teacher had taken her to a strip club?" And he didn't answer that.
<v Luanne Johnson>所以我打电话给制片人安迪·施奈德，我说：“安迪，我对这件事非常不满。”他说：“嗯，你有什么问题？”我说：“我从哪儿开始说起呢？如果你的14岁或15岁女儿放学回家，你发现她的老师带她去了脱衣舞俱乐部，你会高兴吗？”他没有回答。

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:21.610
<v Ira Glass>LuAnne Johnson, the real one, not the TV one, is trying to raise millions of dollars to start a new school these days. And having herself portrayed on national television this way every week, she feels, is not going to help her credibility.
<v Ira Glass>真正的卢安·约翰逊，不是电视里的那个，这些天正努力筹集数百万美元创办一所新学校。她觉得，每周在全国电视上以这种方式被描述，对她的声誉没有任何帮助。

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:44.850
<v Luanne Johnson>They did the hair war to raise funds because Ms. Johnson had a fight with a school board member who it turns out is taking money away from the program. For a teacher to involve kids in a political fight like that among the adults is inappropriate. It's just not professional behavior. And I wouldn't do such a thing. It's undignified, and it's silly. And he said, "Well, I think it's cute." And I said, "Well, I'll tell you what, I'm not cute, and my students don't think I am."
<v Luanne Johnson>他们举办发型战争是为了筹集资金，因为约翰逊老师和一个学校董事会成员吵了一架，结果发现那个人正在从项目中挪用资金。对于老师来说，让孩子卷入这种成年人之间的政治斗争是不合适的。这根本不是职业行为。我也不会做这种事。这有失尊严，而且很傻。他说：“嗯，我觉得这很可爱。”我说：“好吧，我告诉你，我一点也不可爱，我的学生也觉得我不可爱。”

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:50.240
<v Man>Modelled by Academy Program's English teacher, LuAnne Johnson.
<v Man>由学院项目的英语老师卢安·约翰逊模特演示。

00:07:50.240 --> 00:08:06.250
<v Ira Glass>The last scene of the hair war episode has LuAnne Johnson on stage in a strip club modelling a big outrageous hairdo as her students cheer. If LuAnne Johnson made a deal with the devil, it was a devil whose intentions, anyway, seemed particularly benign and well meaning.
<v Ira Glass>发型战争那一集的最后一幕是卢安·约翰逊在脱衣舞俱乐部的舞台上，顶着一个夸张的大发型，学生们在一旁欢呼。如果卢安·约翰逊与魔鬼做了交易，那这个魔鬼的意图看起来倒还算良善且好心。

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:25.570
<v Ira Glass>The producers of Dangerous Minds are Andy Schneider and Diane Frolov. Before this project, they produced the offbeat program Northern Exposure for five years. They've worked together so closely for so long that they habitually finish each other's sentences. And if you ask them what they're trying to accomplish in Dangerous Minds , the goals sound remarkably similar to the ones that LuAnne Johnson had in mind when she wrote her books.
<v Ira Glass>《危险游戏》的制片人是安迪·施奈德和黛安·弗洛洛夫。在这个项目之前，他们制作了五年的另类节目《北国风云》。他们长期密切合作，习惯于接对方的话。如果你问他们试图在《危险游戏》中实现什么，其目标听起来与卢安·约翰逊写书时的初衷惊人地相似。

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:32.110
<v Andy Schneider>To give voice to a lot of kids that don't have a voice in television.
<v Andy Schneider>为许多在电视上没有发言权的孩子发声。

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:38.870
<v Diane Frolov>We set out, we wanted to make people feel that these kids were their own kids. That's the point of the show.
<v Diane Frolov>我们出发时就想，让观众觉得这些孩子就像他们自己的孩子一样。这就是节目的重点。

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:57.510
<v Andy Schneider>These kids who we think of as at-risk kids, kids that come, often, from broken homes or from poverty, that they are not just statistics or something to be feared as elements of crime, but they're just kids like everybody else. They're us. We're them.
<v Andy Schneider>这些被我们视为“高危”的孩子，通常来自破碎家庭或贫困环境，他们不仅仅是统计数字或被视为犯罪因素而令人恐惧，他们只是像其他人一样的孩子。他们就是我们。我们就是他们。

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:16.050
<v Ira Glass>Though the producers do not have a lot of personal experience with these kinds of teenagers, they say they get their storylines from consultants who work with teens like this-- teachers, social workers, a youth gang services counselor. They read books. They got the idea for the hair wars show from a story about hair wars in the Wall Street Journal . And they described LuAnne Johnson's phone call to them about the episode this way.
<v Ira Glass>虽然制片人并没有太多与这类青少年打交道的个人经验，但他们说故事线来源于与这类青少年一起工作的顾问——老师、社会工作者、青少年帮派服务辅导员。他们读书。他们从《华尔街日报》关于发型战争的故事中获得了灵感。他们这样描述卢安·约翰逊关于那一集打来的电话。

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:20.350
<v Andy Schneider>She felt that in the end, when she participated in the hair wars, that she looked--
<v Andy Schneider>她觉得，最后当她参加发型战争时，她看起来——

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:20.750
<v Diane Frolov>Silly.
<v Diane Frolov>傻。

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:44.690
<v Andy Schneider>--silly. And our feeling is, well, that might be the case. But in terms of creating a story, and an arc in the story, and giving a satisfying conclusion, it really helped the story to have her participate in it. And we felt as writers that that was endearing, and something that was positive and really lovable about her, that she would participate in the competition.
<v Andy Schneider>——傻。我们的感觉是，嗯，情况可能确实如此。但就创造故事和故事弧线，以及给出一个令人满意的结局而言，让她参与其中确实对故事有帮助。我们作为编剧觉得那是讨人喜欢的，是她身上积极且可爱的一面，因为她愿意参加这种比赛。

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:53.520
<v Ira Glass>The real LuAnne Johnson also had problems with a scene in an early program where a black character threatens to kill the TV Ms. Johnson because she's encouraging his girlfriend to go to college.
<v Ira Glass>真正的卢安·约翰逊对节目早期的一幕也有意见，在那一幕中，一名黑人角色威胁要杀了电视里的约翰逊老师，因为她在鼓励他的女朋友上大学。

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:03.580
<v Luanne Johnson>And I said, this is so wrong. There are black males who aren't trying to kill people. Why do you have to constantly present them as violent? They can drop out of school even and still not be a criminal.
<v Luanne Johnson>我说，这太不对了。并不是所有黑人男性都在试图杀人。你们为什么要不断地把他们表现为暴力分子？他们即使辍学也未必会成为罪犯。

00:10:03.580 --> 00:10:14.980
<v Luanne Johnson>And I really resent that, because nobody ever threatened me. You know, this is totally unrealistic to think that some black girl's boyfriend's going to come and shoot the teacher because she's trying to tell the girl to go to college.
<v Luanne Johnson>我真的很反感这一点，因为从来没有人威胁过我。你知道，认为某个黑人女孩的男朋友会因为老师劝她上大学就去开枪射杀老师，这太不现实了。

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:29.090
<v Ira Glass>Producers Andy Schneider and Diane Frolov say that, although LuAnne Johnson herself was never threatened, other teachers in her situation certainly have been. And while they don't recall talking to LuAnne Johnson about this particular episode of the show, they don't view the scene the same way she does.
<v Ira Glass>制片人安迪·施奈德和黛安·弗洛洛夫表示，虽然卢安·约翰逊本人从未受到威胁，但处于她这种处境的其他老师肯定遇到过。虽然他们不记得和卢安·约翰逊谈过这一特定剧集，但他们对该场景的看法与她不同。

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:36.270
<v Diane Frolov>The conflict was not so much about the fact that she was going to college but that he was losing control of her.
<v Diane Frolov>冲突的重点不在于她要上大学，而在于他正在失去对她的控制。

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:42.170
<v Andy Schneider>And that dynamic could have happened with white people or anybody.
<v Andy Schneider>这种动态关系可能发生在白人或任何人身上。

00:10:42.170 --> 00:11:02.570
<v Luanne Johnson>Then, one of the things I said to them, and I probably offended them, but I said, "I'm not trying to offend you, but I really believe that one of the problems that the script writers are having with presenting black and Hispanic males accurately is that you don't know any who you haven't mowed your lawn or served your food. You've never been to their houses."
<v Luanne Johnson>然后，我对他们说的一句话——可能冒犯了他们，但我说：“我不是想冒犯你们，但我真心认为，编剧在准确呈现黑人和西班牙裔男性方面遇到的问题之一，是你们除了除草或端菜的人以外，根本不认识其他人。你们从没去过他们的家。”

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:06.730
<v Andy Schneider>I don't know.
<v Andy Schneider>我不知道。

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:13.460
<v Diane Frolov>Are we supposed to list our friends and that sort of thing in our defense?
<v Diane Frolov>我们难道要列出我们的朋友作为防御吗？

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:34.280
<v Andy Schneider>And the process of writing is always a process of research. So, you know, Diane and I wrote Northern Exposure for five years. We didn't know any Alaskans. We didn't know any Native Americans. But the process of writing and getting into a world is a process of researching that and coming into contact with those people that you're trying to write about.
<v Andy Schneider>写作过程总是研究的过程。所以，你知道，黛安和我为《北国风云》写了五年。我们不认识任何阿拉斯加人。我们也不认识任何美洲原住民。但写作和进入一个世界的过程就是研究的过程，通过接触你试图描写的人群来实现。

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.830
<v Diane Frolov>We're not doing this just willy-nilly.
<v Diane Frolov>我们不是随心所欲地这么做。

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:46.560
<v Andy Schneider>And again, I don't think we should have demographics, but our staff is largely minority, our writing staff.
<v Andy Schneider>再说一遍，我不认为我们应该搞人口统计，但我们的员工，包括编剧团队，大部分都是少数族裔。

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.930
<v Diane Frolov>And there are also former teachers on our staff.
<v Diane Frolov>而且我们的员工中也有前教师。

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:55.210
<v Andy Schneider>And mentors and counselors and things like that.
<v Andy Schneider>还有导师、顾问之类的。

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.160
<v Ira Glass>What do you think the overall message of the TV series is?
<v Ira Glass>你认为这部电视剧的整体信息是什么？

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.890
<v Luanne Johnson>Ooh, that it's a war zone.
<v Luanne Johnson>哦，那就是这儿是个战区。

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:04.910
<v Ira Glass>So the overall message of the TV show is, in fact, the exact opposite of the kind of thing you're trying to convey with your books?
<v Ira Glass>所以这部电视剧的整体信息，实际上和你试图通过你的书传达的信息完全相反？

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:12.950
<v Luanne Johnson>Yeah. Yeah. It's not a new story. It's the same old story, which isn't true and never has been.
<v Luanne Johnson>是的。是的。这不是什么新故事。是同样的旧故事，那不是真的，也从来没有真实过。

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:18.820
<v Diane Frolov>Well, we hope that's not the message of our TV series.
<v Diane Frolov>嗯，我们希望那不是我们电视剧的信息。

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:22.090
<v Andy Schneider>We feel that the message of the series is hope.
<v Andy Schneider>我们觉得这部系列剧的信息是希望。

00:12:22.090 --> 00:12:40.270
<v Diane Frolov>Yes, and that people can make a difference in the lives of other people. And that these are kids, and they're, as we've said before, they're our kids. It's very disappointing to hear that from LuAnne.
<v Diane Frolov>是的，人们可以改变他人的生活。正如我们之前所说，这些是孩子，他们是我们的孩子。听到卢安这么说非常令人失望。

00:12:40.270 --> 00:13:03.360
<v Andy Schneider>The war zone aspect of life in school is something that we will concentrate on more, only because you need conflict, you need dramatic situations, you need stakes to be high. Otherwise, I'm not sure how you make a show. I mean, you have to deal with issues that have impact.
<v Andy Schneider>学校生活中的战区视角是我们会更多关注的内容，仅仅是因为你需要冲突，你需要戏剧性的情境，你需要足够高的赌注。否则，我不知道怎么制作一部剧。我的意思是，你必须处理具有影响力的议题。

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:24.430
<v Ira Glass>In a sense, this is the Faustian bargain the producers of Dangerous Minds have made. The high school in their show has more war zone moments, more heroic attempts by teachers to save kids, more crises and near crises than any real inner city high school, because they need three stories per episode, and they have to be dramatic.
<v Ira Glass>在某种意义上，这就是《危险游戏》制片人所做的浮士德交易。他们剧中的高中比起任何真正的内城高中都有更多的战区时刻，更多的教师拯救孩子的英雄壮举，更多的危机和险情，因为他们每一集需要三个故事，而且必须戏剧化。

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:46.750
<v Ira Glass>At this point, LuAnne Johnson says that she's refusing the Faustian bargain offered her by Hollywood. She has no contract with the TV series. They send her checks. She sends them back uncashed. She says if she had it all to do over again, she wouldn't have let anybody make a film or a TV series from her books unless she'd written the screenplay herself. And she's hoping the studio that produces Dangerous Minds , it's Disney, will change the way that it depicts her.
<v Ira Glass>在这一点上，卢安·约翰逊说她拒绝好莱坞向她提供的浮士德交易。她与电视剧没有任何合同。他们给她寄支票，她又把支票退回，没有兑现。她说如果一切可以重来，她绝不会允许任何人把她的书拍成电影或电视剧，除非编剧是她自己。她希望制作《危险游戏》的工作室——迪士尼——能改变对她的刻画方式。

00:13:46.750 --> 00:14:07.830
<v Luanne Johnson>Disney somehow seems to believe that they own LuAnne Johnson the way that they own Mickey Mouse and Dumbo and that they can take this character and make her do what they want and say anything-- you know, whatever experiences they want to have her do. I maintain I'm not a character. And I don't think you should be able to have me say things or do things that I wouldn't do and misrepresent me.
<v Luanne Johnson>迪士尼似乎认为他们像拥有米老鼠和小飞象一样拥有卢安·约翰逊，他们可以拿这个角色做他们想做的事，说他们想说的话——你知道，无论他们想让她经历什么。我坚持认为我不是一个角色。我不认为你们应该让我说出我不会说的话，或者做出我不该做的事，从而歪曲我的形象。

00:14:07.830 --> 00:15:12.720
<v Ira Glass>Of course, as the producers of Dangerous Minds point out, Disney does own a Mickey Mouse and a Dumbo and a LuAnne Johnson. They can make this character do what they want. And given the deals everyone has made, they have to.
<v Ira Glass>当然，正如《危险游戏》的制片人所指出的，迪士尼确实拥有米老鼠、小飞象和卢安·约翰逊。他们可以让这个角色做任何他们想做的事。考虑到每个人都签了合同，他们也只能这么做。

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:34.030
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, The Undiscovered Country. Well, our program today is about deals with the devil, and this next tape seemed oddly fated to end up on the show. Over the last few months, it's been sent to This American Life, not by one, not by two, but by three different radio producers in three different parts of the country, none of them knowing that the other ones were sending it.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，未知的国度。嗯，我们今天的节目是关于与魔鬼的交易，这段录音似乎奇怪地命中注定要出现在节目中。在过去几个月里，它被寄给了《美国生活》，不是被一个人，不是两个人，而是被全美三个不同地方的三位不同的广播制作人寄来的，他们彼此都不知道对方在寄这份录音。

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:56.220
<v Ira Glass>And none of them knew where the tape came from. As best as anybody could reconstruct-- this is quite actually a little story. As best as anybody could reconstruct, NPR commentator Andrei Codrescu was leaving a party in New Orleans, put on his coat, and discovered this tape in his pocket. The tape starts with no explanation, in mid-conversation. Here are some excerpts.
<v Ira Glass>他们没有人知道这份录音来自哪里。据大家拼凑——这其实是一个小故事。据拼凑，NPR评论员安德烈·科德雷斯库在离开新奥尔良的一次派对时，穿上外套，发现口袋里有这份录音。录音没有解释，从对话中间开始。以下是节选。

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.560
<v Woman>She just don't know me that well.
<v Woman>她就是不太了解我。

00:15:57.560 --> 00:15:59.360
<v David Black>She knows you very well.
<v David Black>她很了解你。

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:00.610
<v Woman>Uh-uh. Alice don't know me that well.
<v Woman>不。爱丽丝没那么了解我。

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.220
<v David Black>She said that your mama wants to come back from the dead.
<v David Black>她说你妈妈想从死里复活。

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:04.800
<v Woman>My mama's not dead.
<v Woman>我妈妈没死。

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.430
<v David Black>Well, now you lying or what? You tell me the truth now.
<v David Black>好吧，那你是在撒谎吗？现在你得跟我说实话。

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:13.030
<v Woman>I don't have a reason to lie to you, mister, and especially about my mama.
<v Woman>我没理由对你撒谎，先生，尤其是在关于我妈妈的事情上。

00:16:13.030 --> 00:16:23.810
<v David Black>I know, I know. But now here's the thing, now. I deal in mysticism. I bring back dead people. Is there anybody dead that you would like to bring back?
<v David Black>我知道，我知道。但现在是这样，我从事神秘学。我能让人死而复生。有没有什么死人你想带回来的？

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:26.050
<v Woman>No, uh-uh.
<v Woman>不，不。

00:16:26.050 --> 00:16:27.590
<v David Black>Anybody at all?
<v David Black>谁都行吗？

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:35.110
<v Woman>No. I had told [? Mr. Slips ?] just now that I had wanted to talk to him about it.
<v Woman>不。我刚才告诉过[？斯利普先生？]我想和他谈谈这件事。

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:36.580
<v David Black>Have you ever seen a spirit?
<v David Black>你见过灵魂吗？

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:37.440
<v Woman>No.
<v Woman>没有。

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:38.320
<v David Black>You want to?
<v David Black>你想见吗？

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:39.300
<v Woman>No.
<v Woman>不想。

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:40.120
<v David Black>What?
<v David Black>什么？

00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:40.930
<v Woman>No.
<v Woman>不想。

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:41.610
<v David Black>Why?
<v David Black>为什么？

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:45.160
<v Woman>I just don't want to see none. I have no reason to want to see one.
<v Woman>我就是不想见任何灵魂。我没理由想见。

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:46.910
<v David Black>Madam, can I come over to your house tonight?
<v David Black>夫人，我今晚能去你家吗？

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:48.020
<v Woman>Uh-uh. No. No.
<v Woman>不。不。不。

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:53.310
<v David Black>I want to come over and talk to you and bring a spirit along with me.
<v David Black>我想去你那儿，和你谈谈，还想带个灵魂去。

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:56.310
<v Woman>Well, you leave that spirit where it at, and you stay where you at.
<v Woman>好吧，你把那灵魂留在它待着的地方，你自己也待在你待着的地方。

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:57.800
<v David Black>But it's not a mean spirit.
<v David Black>但它不是个恶灵。

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:00.900
<v Woman>I don't want no kind of spirit.
<v Woman>我不需要任何灵魂。

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.240
<v David Black>I won't charge you a cent.
<v David Black>我一分钱都不会收你的。

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:12.240
<v Woman>I don't want to see no spirit. I don't want to be bothered with no spirit. I don't want no spirit around me. If I see a spirit, I wouldn't be able to sleep for months.
<v Woman>我不想见什么灵魂。我不想被灵魂打扰。我不想身边有灵魂。如果我见到灵魂，我几个月都睡不着觉。

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:13.530
<v David Black>Do you believe in spirits?
<v David Black>你相信灵魂吗？

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.240
<v Woman>No. I believe, let me tell you, I believe--
<v Woman>不。我相信，让我告诉你，我相信——

00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:17.540
<v David Black>I'll bring it over and show him to you tonight.
<v David Black>我今晚带它过来给你看看。

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:18.520
<v Woman>Uh-uh, I don't want to see him.
<v Woman>不，我不想见它。

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:19.640
<v David Black>I'm coming tonight.
<v David Black>我今晚会去。

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:30.670
<v Woman>You ain't coming to my house tonight.
<v Woman>你今晚别想来我家。

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:32.540
<v David Black>You don't want to see the spirit?
<v David Black>你不想见这个灵魂吗？

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:34.260
<v Woman>No, I don't want to see no spirit.
<v Woman>不，我不想见什么灵魂。

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.950
<v David Black>Sister, there's nothing to get frightened of.
<v David Black>姐妹，没什么好害怕的。

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.580
<v Woman>Uh-uh, I don't want to see no spirit.
<v Woman>不，我不想见什么灵魂。

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:38.340
<v David Black>Why?
<v David Black>为什么？

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:47.890
<v Woman>Because I said I don't want to see no spirit. And you see, firstly, Alice lied to you. She really did. If she told you that, she's lying.
<v Woman>因为我说过我不想见什么灵魂。而且你看，首先，爱丽丝对你撒了谎。她真的撒了谎。如果她那么说，她在撒谎。

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.050
<v David Black>Alice belongs to the occult.
<v David Black>爱丽丝属于神秘学团体。

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.830
<v Woman>What is that?
<v Woman>那是什么？

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:55.610
<v David Black>Alice is one of our members of the spirits.
<v David Black>爱丽丝是我们灵魂组织的成员之一。

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:56.560
<v Woman>Alice is a spirit?
<v Woman>爱丽丝是个灵魂？

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:03.620
<v David Black>She is a member of our spirit group. In other words, Alice can bring the spirits back.
<v David Black>她是我们的灵魂小组成员。换句话说，爱丽丝能把灵魂带回来。

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:06.040
<v Woman>What do you mean? She can bring the spirit back where?
<v Woman>你是什么意思？她能把灵魂带到哪儿去？

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:09.340
<v David Black>She can bring them back from the dead.
<v David Black>她能让他们从死里复活。

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.500
<v Woman>Oh, she can?
<v Woman>哦，真的吗？

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.730
<v David Black>I'll have her come over with me tonight, too.
<v David Black>我也让她今晚和我一起过来。

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:17.540
<v Woman>You are not coming to my house, and Alice isn't either.
<v Woman>你别想来我家，爱丽丝也不行。

00:18:17.540 --> 00:18:19.520
<v David Black>Well, do you like Alice, or what?
<v David Black>好吧，你喜欢爱丽丝吗，还是怎么着？

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:26.930
<v Woman>I have nothing against Alice. She is a very, very sweet person. She is a good neighbor. It is nothing in the world. I don't believe--
<v Woman>我对爱丽丝没什么意见。她是一个非常、非常贴心的人。她是个好邻居。这世上没什么大不了的。我不相信——

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:30.800
<v David Black>Well, we'll be over about 7:30, 8:00. As soon as it gets dark.
<v David Black>好吧，我们大概7:30、8:00左右过去。天一黑我们就去。

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:32.710
<v Woman>Well, I bet you you won't come in my house.
<v Woman>好吧，我敢说你进不了我家门。

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:34.270
<v David Black>Well, you don't like Alice? Or what is it?
<v David Black>好吧，你不喜欢爱丽丝吗？还是怎么回事？

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:35.200
<v Woman>I love Alice.
<v Woman>我爱爱丽丝。

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:36.230
<v David Black>You don't trust us?
<v David Black>你不信任我们？

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:50.250
<v Woman>I don't trust the spirit.
<v Woman>我不信任灵魂。

00:18:50.250 --> 00:18:51.730
<v David Black>Will you pray with me?
<v David Black>你愿意和我一起祈祷吗？

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:52.720
<v Woman>When?
<v Woman>什么时候？

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:53.710
<v David Black>Now.
<v David Black>现在。

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:55.560
<v Woman>Pray, go ahead and pray.
<v Woman>祈祷，去祈祷吧。

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.250
<v David Black>Repeat after me. Oh  lord--
<v David Black>跟我重复。哦，主啊——

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:58.690
<v Woman>Uh-uh.
<v Woman>不。

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:00.360
<v David Black>Say a prayer with me.
<v David Black>跟我一起祷告。

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:01.670
<v Woman>Uh-uh.
<v Woman>不。

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.400
<v David Black>I want to put a hex on you.
<v David Black>我想对你下咒。

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:03.750
<v Woman>Uh-uh.
<v Woman>不。

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:09.560
<v David Black>Oh lord-- say it with me. Is Alice over there?
<v David Black>哦，主啊——跟我一起说。爱丽丝在那儿吗？

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:10.750
<v Woman>Uh-uh.
<v Woman>不。

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.830
<v David Black>Tell her to come talk to me.
<v David Black>告诉她来跟我谈谈。

