<v Ira Glass>全是浮士德。这座城市简直浮士德成狂了。所以我们想，我们应该赶上这波撒旦潮流。这就是我们这一小时要做的。今天的节目就是关于与魔鬼做交易，出卖灵魂。

00:26:38.990 --> 00:27:11.950
<v Ira Glass>We are at Act Three of our program, Voyager. In the opera Faust, when the devil appears for the first time, he says, simply, "Here I am. Why are you surprised?" See, that is the way to do it. That is the way to do that. The problem in our daily lives is that when we're offered the deal with the devil, we often do not know that that is the choice we're making. This next story is about a bargain like that. This American Life producer Alix Spiegel headed home to Baltimore to meet with a friend of hers, Jayna Park.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第三幕，《航海家》。在歌剧《浮士德》中，当魔鬼第一次出现时，他简单地说：“我在这儿。你为什么惊讶？”看，那才是正确的做法。这才是该有的样子。现实生活中的问题是，当我们被提供与魔鬼的交易时，我们往往不知道这就是我们所做的选择。下一个故事就是关于这样的交易。《美国生活》制作人阿丽克斯·斯皮格尔回到了巴尔的摩，去见她的朋友杰娜·帕克。

00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:25.970
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna and I meet in the courtyard of the Peabody Conservatory of Music, then walk down the street to Louis's Bookstore Cafe. When we were teenagers, Jayna and I would go to Louis's between orchestra and violin lessons. We'd eat fish sandwiches, talk about music, and gawk at the art boys.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜和我约在皮博迪音乐学院的庭院见面，然后走下街去路易斯书店咖啡馆。我们十几岁的时候，杰娜和我会在管弦乐课和钢琴课之间去路易斯咖啡馆。我们吃鱼肉三明治，谈论音乐，盯着艺术系男生看。

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:47.890
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna is Korean. When Jayna was 11, Jayna, her mother, her violin teacher, and her aunt, all drove four hours from the small town where she grew up to Seoul, South Korea's capital. They went to a big hotel, the hotel where all the Korean celebrities and international tourists stayed, and went to the room of violinist Berl Senofsky. She played him the Mendelssohn violin concerto.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜是韩国人。杰娜11岁那年，她和母亲、小提琴老师以及姨妈一起，开车四个小时从她长大的小镇赶往韩国首都首尔。他们去了一家大酒店，那是韩国名人和国际游客住的酒店，然后去了小提琴家伯尔·塞诺夫斯基的房间。她为他演奏了门德尔松小提琴协奏曲。

00:27:47.890 --> 00:28:24.630
<v Jayna Park>I think the first thing he said was-- well, the first thing that my aunt translated to us was-- she should come to the States and study with me. Is that possible? Is that possible? Like, would your family allow her to go? Asking my mother this, basically. And I remember my mother just being like, what? What? What? You want what? You know, like she was just utterly confused. And then at the same time, I could see her just like sort of smiling. Like she couldn't believe her daughter, you know, whatever.
<v Jayna Park>我想他说的第一件事是——嗯，我姨妈翻译给我们的第一件事是——她应该来美国跟我学习。这可能吗？这可能吗？比如，你的家人会允许她去吗？基本上是在问我母亲。我记得我母亲当时就像是，什么？什么？什么？你要什么？你知道，她完全困惑了。但同时，我能看到她脸上露出某种微笑。好像她无法相信自己的女儿，你知道，等等。

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:34.070
<v Alix Spiegel>No one in Jayna's family really knew what she'd be getting into if she accepted Senofsky's offer to go to America. But it was clear from the beginning that if Jayna went, she would go alone.
<v Alix Spiegel>如果接受塞诺夫斯基去美国的邀请，杰娜的家人没人真正知道她会陷入什么境地。但从一开始就很清楚，如果杰娜去，她将独自前往。

00:28:34.070 --> 00:29:13.070
<v Jayna Park>My dad was talking about, you know, maybe we might have to give her up for adoption. I kind of almost remember feeling like, he's asking me, like, would I be willing to give up my parents and my family for this glamorous life, or whatever? And at the time I didn't understand. I was like, how can you ask me this question? I don't want to give up anything.
<v Jayna Park>我父亲谈论的是，你知道，也许我们得把她送去领养。我几乎记得当时的感觉，他像是在问我，如果你愿意为了这种光鲜亮丽的生活放弃父母和家庭吗？当时我不明白。我就想，你怎么能问我这个问题？我不想放弃任何东西。

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:25.700
<v Alix Spiegel>Later, at Jayna's house, she brings out the violin. I ask her to play me the Mendelssohn, the song that she played for Mr. Senofsky, the song that literally divided her life into past and present. The opening melody is famous for being hard to play.
<v Alix Spiegel>后来，在杰娜家，她拿出了小提琴。我请她为我演奏门德尔松，就是那首她为塞诺夫斯基先生演奏的曲子，那首把她的生活一分为二的曲子。开头的旋律因演奏难度极高而闻名。

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:39.380
<v Jayna Park>[INAUDIBLE] [PLAYING VIOLIN]
<v Jayna Park>[听不清] [演奏小提琴]

00:29:39.380 --> 00:30:05.220
<v Alix Spiegel>I remember the first time I saw Jayna. She was short and chubby, and dressed in a red Korean coat. Immediately after coming to America, Jayna dropped about 20 pounds. I remember people whispering that she was chubby when she arrived because meals were the only time that Jayna's strict Korean mother would allow her to stop practicing. There were other rumors, all part of the Jayna mystique, the young Korean prodigy who had sacrificed so much, come alone from Korea to study in Baltimore.
<v Alix Spiegel>我还记得第一次见到杰娜的情景。她又矮又胖，穿着一件红色的韩国大衣。来到美国后，杰娜立刻瘦了20磅左右。我记得人们悄悄议论说她刚到时胖是因为吃饭是杰娜严厉的韩国母亲唯一允许她停止练琴的时候。还有其他传言，都是杰娜神秘色彩的一部分，那位牺牲了这么多、独自从韩国来到巴尔的摩学习的年轻韩国神童。

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:28.660
<v Alix Spiegel>Classical music, like ballet or gymnastics, is a discipline with a torrentious aesthetic. The greater the sacrifice, the bigger the hero. And to Peabody, a second-rate music school in a second-rate town, Jayna's sacrifice and talent were Olympian. We were all awed. No one so young played so well.
<v Alix Spiegel>古典音乐，就像芭蕾或体操一样，是一种有着痛苦审美的学科。牺牲越大，英雄越伟大。对于皮博迪这个二流城镇里的二流音乐学院来说，杰娜的牺牲和天赋简直是奥林匹克级别的。我们都感到敬畏。没人能像她这么小年纪就弹得这么好。

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:41.780
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna moved in with a host family, the Kilsteads. Carla Kilstead, another Senofsky student, was about the same age as Jayna and I. We would all hang out together, play Space Invaders in the Peabody lounge and beg money off our moms for the candy machines.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜搬进了一个寄宿家庭，基尔斯特德一家。卡拉·基尔斯特德是另一名塞诺夫斯基的学生，和杰娜还有我差不多大。我们常在一起玩，在皮博迪休息室玩《太空入侵者》，然后向我们的妈妈讨钱去买自动贩卖机的糖果。

00:30:41.780 --> 00:30:46.960
<v Alix Spiegel>Then I remember you being in the car with my mom, and me, and Carla.
<v Alix Spiegel>然后我记得你跟我妈妈、我还有卡拉在车里。

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:48.780
<v Jayna Park>Was this like the first time ever that I came to Peabody?
<v Jayna Park>这是我第一次来皮博迪吗？

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:54.350
<v Alix Spiegel>This was the first time ever. And I remember we were driving past the Inner Harbor, and there was a ship.
<v Alix Spiegel>这是第一次。我记得当时我们开车经过内港，那里有一艘船。

00:30:54.350 --> 00:31:11.580
<v Jayna Park>Ship. That was my first curse word, because, yeah, you and Carla were trying to teach me. Like, you guys just took me by the hand, OK, look, see, that's a ship. And I was like, ah, ah, [BLEEP], [BLEEP].
<v Jayna Park>船。那是我的第一个脏话，因为，是的，你和卡拉当时在教我。就像，你们带我手把手教学，OK，看，看见没，那是一艘船。我就说，啊，啊，[消音]，[消音]。

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:35.490
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna says the first couple of years in America were everything she wanted them to be. Everything was new. Everything was interesting. Her life with her host family was good. She did well in competitions, and Senofsky got his manager to set up some concerts for her in Holland. Then, after two years of study at Peabody, Jayna went back to Korea for the first time. And when she came back to America, things started to fall apart. Her relationship with her host family deteriorated.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜说在美国的头几年，她想要的一切都实现了。一切都是新鲜的。一切都很令人感兴趣。她和寄宿家庭的生活也很融洽。她在比赛中表现出色，塞诺夫斯基让他的经纪人为她在荷兰安排了一些音乐会。之后，在皮博迪学习两年后，杰娜第一次回到了韩国。当她回到美国时，事情开始分崩离析。她和寄宿家庭的关系恶化了。

00:31:35.490 --> 00:32:12.900
<v Jayna Park>I don't know what it was, but I think like around the third year, or something like that that I was living with them, I really started to feel like my presence there was a burden to them, you know? And that's when it started getting hard, because then I realized, oh my gosh, what am I doing? I've been here, I've been living with a family that's not mine, basically strangers. What can they possibly like about this?
<v Jayna Park>我不知道是什么原因，但我觉得大概在第三年左右，我住在那儿的时候，我真的开始觉得我的存在对他们来说是一个负担，你知道吗？那时生活开始变得困难，因为我意识到，天哪，我在做什么？我一直住在一个不是我家的家庭里，基本上是陌生人家里。他们怎么可能喜欢这一点？

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:26.290
<v Alix Spiegel>She left that host family and moved in with another and then left them for another. More was expected of Jayna than of any of us, and she was essentially raising herself. She was lonely and started to miss her family in Korea. At one point, she called her mom.
<v Alix Spiegel>她离开了那个寄宿家庭，搬到了另一个，然后又离开那个去了下一个。人们对杰娜的期望比对我们任何人都高，而她实际上是在独自抚养自己。她很孤独，开始想念在韩国的家人。有一次，她给母亲打电话。

00:32:26.290 --> 00:32:56.830
<v Jayna Park>Well, I was really upset. So I called and I was crying, and I said, you know, can I just come back and just go back to school there? At least let me try that and see if that works. And I was really hoping, if not assuming, that my mother would just say, sure, come back, you know?
<v Jayna Park>嗯，我当时真的很沮丧。所以我打电话去哭着说，你知道，我能回去在那边上学吗？至少让我试一下看看行不行。我当时真的希望，如果不是认定的话，我母亲会说，当然，回来吧，你知道吗？

00:32:56.830 --> 00:33:19.730
<v Jayna Park>She said, that's ridiculous. You've been there for so long now, and now you have to carry on what you've been doing. And I just remember hanging up. I don't remember exactly what I said. I just remember hanging up, feeling so desperate.
<v Jayna Park>她说，这很荒唐。你在那边已经待了这么久了，现在你必须继续做你一直在做的事情。我记得我挂了电话。我不记得确切说了什么。只记得挂断电话时，感觉无比绝望。

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:44.790
<v Alix Spiegel>I remember Jayna during this part of her life. I remember driving her back from orchestra rehearsal and sitting with her in my car outside her house. I was 16 and she was 14. I remember her telling me about the problems in her family back home in Korea. Her father was having financial difficulties. Her brother was having trouble getting into the right college, which in Korea determines your whole future. And her sister had had some kind of breakdown and had been put into a mental institution.
<v Alix Spiegel>我记得杰娜生命中的那段时间。我记得送她从管弦乐排练回家，和她坐在我家外面的车里。我16岁，她14岁。我记得她告诉我韩国老家家庭出的问题。父亲经济困难。弟弟在申请合适的大学时遇到麻烦，这在韩国决定了整个未来。妹妹似乎精神崩溃，被关进了精神病院。

00:33:44.790 --> 00:34:06.290
<v Alix Spiegel>It was all very draining, and Jayna was having a hard time. But what I remember best was that these crises somehow forced Jayna to think about her decision to come to America in a new way. Watching her family struggle from a distance somehow brought her face to face with her own distance from them and from her culture. I remember her telling me that she felt she could never go back to Korea, that she had missed too much.
<v Alix Spiegel>这一切非常令人心力交瘁，杰娜的日子很难过。但我记忆最深的是，这些危机迫使杰娜以一种新的方式思考她来美国的决定。从远处看着家人挣扎，使她不得不直面自己与家人、与文化的距离。我记得她告诉我，她觉得她再也回不去韩国了，她错过了太多。

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:41.820
<v Jayna Park>I was accused constantly by my family of becoming so Americanized. But then I would look at myself here, and I would be hanging out with my friends and stuff. And then all of a sudden, I would look at myself and just feel like, gosh, but I'm not like them. I'm not one of them. So I really just kind of felt like I didn't belong anywhere. And that's when I slept a lot.
<v Jayna Park>我的家人不断指责我变得太美国化了。但当我在这里审视自己时，我会和朋友一起混什么的。然后突然间，我会看着自己，觉得，天哪，但我跟他们不一样。我不是他们中的一员。所以我真的觉得我哪儿都不属于。那时我睡了很多觉。

00:34:41.820 --> 00:34:55.390
<v Alix Spiegel>In the world of classical music, if you don't make it as a soloist by the time you're 16 or 17, odds are you're never going to make it. At 20, it hadn't happened for Jayna. She was still practicing and competing, but it was more of a struggle.
<v Alix Spiegel>在古典音乐界，如果你在16或17岁之前没有成为独奏家，那你可能永远也不会成功。在20岁时，杰娜还没能做到。她还在练习和比赛，但那更像是一场挣扎。

00:34:55.390 --> 00:35:55.750
<v Jayna Park>And so one day, I just decided, OK, I'm going to try something else. I want to know what it's like to not be a violinist. So I put my violin away, and for two weeks, every day for two weeks, I was just living like a non-violinist. I was going to bookstores, looking for-- I don't know. I was reading, just trying to look for what interests me. I was just like wanting-- I don't know. I was dwelling on that luxury of being able to choose for myself. But eventually, I really missed it. I really missed the music.
<v Jayna Park>所以有一天，我决定，OK，我要尝试别的东西。我想知道不是小提琴家是什么感觉。所以我把小提琴收了起来，在那两周里，每天我都像个非小提琴家一样生活。我去了书店，寻找——我不知道。我在阅读，试图寻找什么让我感兴趣。我只是想要——我不知道。我沉溺于那种能为自己做选择的奢侈中。但最终，我真的想念它。我真的想念音乐。

00:35:55.750 --> 00:36:13.110
<v Alix Spiegel>Someone recently wrote that the tragedy of Bob Dole is not only that he sold out his principles and sold out everything he ever believed in to become president, this deficit hawk who campaigned on the kind of tax cut he used to ridicule as voodoo economics, but that after he sold out his principles and his soul, he discovered that there were no buyers.
<v Alix Spiegel>最近有人写道，鲍勃·多尔的悲剧不仅在于他为了成为总统而出卖了自己的原则、出卖了一切他曾经相信的东西，这个曾经嘲笑税收减免是巫术经济学的赤字鹰派现在却竞选税收减免，还在于他在出卖原则和灵魂后，发现根本没有买家。

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:41.380
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna's story reminds me of that. She agrees that yes, she made a Faustian bargain. She left her family, left her country, left her home. But she never got the fame and fortune she made all those sacrifices for. And when I talked to her, I somehow expected her to be as conflicted about it as she was when we were in my car at 16. But in fact, she was fine with it. Her feelings have changed, and she's outgrown the premise of the bargain. She doesn't particularly want to be a soloist, and she's not driven for fame.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜的故事让我想起这一点。她同意，是的，她做了一笔浮士德式的交易。她离开了家人，离开了国家，离开了家园。但她并没有得到她为之做出所有牺牲的名利。当我跟她交谈时，我本以为她会像我们16岁坐在车里时那样纠结。但实际上，她对此感到还好。她的感受改变了，她已经超脱了交易的初衷。她并不是特别想成为独奏家，她也不追求名声。

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:58.940
<v Alix Spiegel>With or without violin, Jayna clearly prefers life in America to the restricted life she would have had in Korea. And though recently her parents have started talking about coming to the States, moving in with her so that they can be closer, she finds these conversations make her nervous. She doesn't want to give up the independence she's had since she was 11.
<v Alix Spiegel>无论有没有小提琴，杰娜显然更喜欢在美国的生活，而不是如果留在韩国会受到的那种限制生活。虽然最近她的父母开始谈论来美国，搬来和她住，这样可以离得近点，但她发现这些对话让她感到紧张。她不想放弃从11岁起就拥有的独立。

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:32.360
<v Jayna Park>I don't feel regret. I don't feel regret that he was there, and that I gave in to this deal. I don't know if I should say I gave in. But I really don't regret the fact that he was there that day, listening to me and saying those things, you know, come with me to a new place, a new world, and all that. I don't think that he was wrong.
<v Jayna Park>我不后悔。我不后悔他在那里，我不后悔我答应了这笔交易。我不知道我是不是该说我妥协了。但我真的不后悔那天他在那里，听我演奏并说出那些话，你知道，带我到一个新的地方、新的世界之类的。我不认为他错了。

00:37:32.360 --> 00:37:55.100
<v Jayna Park>Because I have to say, it was just a very adventurous thing to do. And I really feel like if I had a choice to stay home and to do this, I think every time, I would choose it, choose to go.
<v Jayna Park>因为我得说，这确实是一件非常有冒险精神的事。我真的觉得，如果我有机会留在家还是做这个，我觉得每次我都会选它，选去那里。

00:37:55.100 --> 00:38:37.170
<v Alix Spiegel>Jayna's now 23. She's still at Peabody. She gigs with the Lyric Opera Orchestra and Young Concert Artists' Orchestra in Baltimore. She has a sponsor, a businessman in North Carolina who heard her perform and now helps her with a monthly check. At her apartment, after playing three songs for me, Jayna stops and is about to put her violin away. She asks, should I keep playing? And I say, sure, play more. No, she says, I mean the violin. Jayna's still wondering.
<v Alix Spiegel>杰娜现在23岁。她还在皮博迪。她在巴尔的摩的抒情歌剧管弦乐团和青年音乐家管弦乐团进行演出。她有一位赞助人，一位北卡罗来纳州的商人，他听过她表演，现在每月寄支票帮她。在她的公寓，为我演奏了三首歌后，杰娜停了下来，正准备把小提琴收起来。她问，我还要继续演奏吗？我说，当然，多演奏点。不，她说，我是指小提琴。杰娜还在犹豫。

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:39.760
<v Ira Glass>Act Four, Carmen Becomes Faust.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，卡门变身浮士德。

00:38:39.760 --> 00:39:03.360
<v Carmen Delzell>When I was nearing my 30th birthday, I decided it was about time to get off welfare. Both the children were in school, and old dreams of being a hippie revolutionary scrounging for a pound of bulgur and soybeans in the mountains didn't seem nearly as romantic as it had five years before. My husband had run off to the Virgin Islands two winters before with a fat Jewish girl who knew somebody down there who had a boat.
<v Carmen Delzell>当我快要满30岁时，我决定是时候摆脱福利金生活了。两个孩子都在上学，而当嬉皮士革命者在山里搜寻一磅干小麦和大豆的旧梦，看起来远没有五年前那么浪漫。我丈夫在两个冬天前带着一个犹太胖女孩私奔到了维尔京群岛，那女孩在那边有熟人还有条船。

00:39:03.360 --> 00:39:30.740
<v Ira Glass>When this story begins, this story of another deal with the devil, another person coming in from the sticks to the big city, Carmen Delzell is living in a tenant farmer's shack, no phone or radio or clock. Water comes from a pump, is heated on an Ashley wood stove. It's 1978. And to earn her way out of there, Carmen Delzell started to go to auctions and thrift stores out in the country, and buy stuff cheap that she could drive into Washington, DC and sell for higher prices at the flea markets there.
<v Ira Glass>在这个故事开始时，这个关于另一个与魔鬼交易的故事，关于另一个人从乡下进大城市的故事，卡门·德尔泽尔住在一个佃农小屋里，没有电话、收音机或钟表。水是从水泵抽出来的，在阿什利木炉上烧热。那是1978年。为了赚点钱摆脱那里，卡门·德尔泽尔开始去乡下的拍卖会和旧货店，买些廉价的东西，然后开车拉到华盛顿特区，在跳蚤市场上卖出更高的价格。

00:39:30.740 --> 00:39:47.200
<v Carmen Delzell>I must have been a sight in those days with my folding tables and clothes racks and raggedy looking children in Georgetown. I think I was pretty then, but I didn't know it. I wore peasant blouses with no brassiere, and my hair was long and curly and white-blond from the sun.
<v Carmen Delzell>在那段日子里，我带着折叠桌、衣架和衣衫褴褛的孩子们在乔治敦，一定很引人注目。我觉得我当时很美，但我自己不知道。我穿着农民衬衫，不戴胸罩，头发长而卷曲，被太阳晒成了白金色。

00:39:47.200 --> 00:40:11.230
<v Carmen Delzell>You must realize that in those days, hippie women were really different from straight women. We thought we were practically another race. We believed in all kinds of dogmas and rhetoric. We believed it, and we lived it. Not eating meat was one of those. Smoking dope was really important. We believed in karma and revolution and a whole lot of obscure practices which sound silly now but made a whole lot of sense back then.
<v Carmen Delzell>你必须明白，在那些日子里，嬉皮女性和正统女性真的非常不同。我们认为我们几乎是另一个物种。我们相信各种各样的教条和修辞。我们相信它，并践行它。不吃肉是其中之一。抽大麻真的非常重要。我们相信业力、革命和一大堆现在听起来很傻但在当时却非常有意义的晦涩做法。

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:37.320
<v Carmen Delzell>So when a young lawyer or real estate broker or something would come along and hang around too long, I didn't know what to think. Didn't they know that we were worlds apart in every way? What could we possibly have to say to each other? I was so naive. When the man who ran the flea market invited me out to dinner at the Steak 'n Egg Kitchen, I was flabbergasted. I was, but I went anyway.
<v Carmen Delzell>所以当一个年轻的律师或房地产经纪人什么的走过来，还在那儿晃悠太久时，我不知道该想什么。难道他们不知道我们在各个方面都来自不同的世界吗？我们有什么话可说？我真是太天真了。当跳蚤市场的负责人邀请我去Steak 'n Egg Kitchen吃晚饭时，我简直惊呆了。我是，但我还是去了。

00:40:37.320 --> 00:41:03.380
<v Carmen Delzell>By now, it was beginning to get chilly. Money would really be tight when it got too cold to sell on the street, so I decided to go to a fortune teller. She lived and worked in a storefront in Chinatown. She was fat and sneaky-looking and had only one breast. But as it turned out, she was right about everything to come, and she gave me three wishes. I wished for an apartment, a man, and an antique store.
<v Carmen Delzell>到这时候，天开始变凉了。一旦冷到无法在街上摆摊，钱就会非常紧，所以我决定去找个算命先生。她在唐人街的一个店面里工作。她很胖，看起来很狡猾，只有一只乳房。但事实证明，关于后来发生的一切，她都说对了，她还给了我三个愿望。我许愿要一套公寓、一个男人和一家古董店。

00:41:03.380 --> 00:41:27.580
<v Carmen Delzell>She gave me a strange list of things to buy. They were a bottle of Chinese floor wash, four seven-day candles, red, white, blue, and green, a box of bano sangrados, which means spirit bath, white flowers, jinx remover powder, and Indian money draw soap. I drove back to the country $20 poorer and kind of depressed that I could have been so vulnerable.
<v Carmen Delzell>她给了我一张奇怪的购物单。那是：一瓶中国地板清洗液，四根七天蜡烛（红、白、蓝、绿），一盒bano sangrados（意为精神沐浴液），白花，去晦粉，和印度“引财”肥皂。我开车回乡下，少了20美元，而且因为自己竟然这么容易受骗而感到有些沮丧。

00:41:27.580 --> 00:41:53.710
<v Carmen Delzell>That was on a Saturday. On Sunday, I poured the bottle of Chinese floor wash into the bucket of fresh pumped water and scrubbed all the floors in the house with it. This was to clear out evil spirits. Then when the children went to sleep, I began the ritual of the spirit bath. I placed the candles around the galvanized tin tub that we used for everything, filled it with kettles of hot water, and poured in the first bottle of red liquid from the box I bought.
<v Carmen Delzell>那是星期六。星期天，我把那瓶中国地板清洗液倒进刚抽上来的水桶里，用它把房子的所有地板都擦了一遍。这是为了清理邪灵。然后当孩子们睡着后，我开始了精神沐浴的仪式。我把蜡烛摆在那只我们用来做任何事的镀锌铁桶周围，倒满水壶烧的热水，倒入从店里买来的红色瓶子里的第一份液体。

00:41:53.710 --> 00:42:10.860
<v Carmen Delzell>Just before I got in the tub, I arranged the white roses in front of my dead mother's picture. This was to ensure her blessing and ask her to intervene on my behalf. As soon as I got in the water, I got this weird, tingly feeling. The shadows from the candles loomed around me like ghosts, and the water looked like blood.
<v Carmen Delzell>就在我进木桶前，我把白玫瑰插在死去的母亲的照片前。这是为了确保她的祝福，并请求她代表我进行干预。当我一进水里，就有一种奇怪的刺痛感。蜡烛映出的影子像鬼魂一样在我周围徘徊，水看起来像血一样。

00:42:10.860 --> 00:42:40.050
<v Carmen Delzell>I sat there naked and shivering by the wood stove and prayed out loud for God to send me things I associated with red. I asked for sex, adventure, and central heat. I also asked for red cowboy boots and a ruby ring. On Monday morning, I drove to Culpepper and found the cowboy boots for $2 at the Pink Ladies Shop and the ring in a jar of buttons which they let me have for $0.25. That night, the awful man from the flea market drove out to see me and spent the night.
<v Carmen Delzell>我赤身裸体、瑟瑟发抖地坐在木炉旁，大声祈祷上帝赐给我与红色相关的东西。我要求性、冒险和中央供暖。我还要求红色牛仔靴和红宝石戒指。星期一早上，我开车去库尔佩珀，在粉红女士商店花2美元买到了牛仔靴，又在一个装满纽扣的罐子里找到了戒指，他们只收了我0.25美元。那天晚上，跳蚤市场的那个讨厌男人开车来看我，并在那儿待了一晚。

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:57.620
<v Carmen Delzell>I realized my next wishes would have to be more specific. Tuesday evening, I repeated the ritual, except the water turned blue. I found it a harder color to match with things I needed, so I just asked for general good fortune. Nothing special happened Wednesday, but nothing went wrong either.
<v Carmen Delzell>我意识到接下来的愿望必须更具体。星期二晚上，我重复了仪式，除了水变成了蓝色。我发现这个颜色很难与我需要的东西相匹配，所以我只是求了笼统的“好运”。星期三没什么特别的事情发生，但也什么都没出错。

00:42:57.620 --> 00:43:34.380
<v Carmen Delzell>Well, it went on like that for the next 14 days. My luck began to turn. I found better clothes in the thrift shops than I'd ever found before, and made over $100 at the flea market. My landlord began to smile and wave at me when I drove past his house, and letters with small checks in them arrived at the post office. But I guess I have to get to the unbelievable part, and here's what it is. A handsome young millionaire came up to me the last day of the baths and offered me free rent in a sunny slum apartment, and $10,000 to start my own business. Sounds like a lie, doesn't it? I know it does, but it's true.
<v Carmen Delzell>嗯，接下来的14天里都是这样。我的运气开始好转。我在旧货店里找到了比以往任何时候都好的衣服，在跳蚤市场上赚了100多美元。我的房东开始在我开车经过他家时冲我微笑和挥手，邮局里也收到了带有小额支票的信件。但我猜我必须说到那不可思议的部分了，事实就是：最后一次洗浴那天，一个英俊的年轻百万富翁走到我面前，主动提出让我免费住进一间阳光充足的贫民公寓，还给我1万美元创业。听起来像谎言，对吧？我知道是，但这是真的。

00:43:34.380 --> 00:44:02.310
<v Carmen Delzell>Soon after we moved there, he fell in love with me, and we all lived together that winter, sleeping on mattresses on the floor and eating Chinese food out of cardboard containers. That was one of the happiest times in my life, though I missed certain things about living alone in the country. The dark side of the magic hadn't revealed itself yet, so we blithely enjoyed our warm rooms, and I thanked Jesus every night for his blessings.
<v Carmen Delzell>搬过去不久，他爱上了我，那个冬天我们住在一起，睡在地板的床垫上，吃着纸盒装的中餐。那是我一生中最快乐的时光之一，尽管我怀念独自住在乡下的一些东西。魔法的阴暗面还没显现，所以我们无忧无虑地享受着温暖的房间，我每晚都感谢耶稣的祝福。

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:25.090
<v Carmen Delzell>I've heard that we punish ourselves better and more profoundly than any spirits or gods we can imagine. I hope that's true. I hope that's true because I still feel guilty. Because, you see, David, the millionaire, was married, and I knew it from the very beginning. He invited me to Washington, DC to have dinner with him and his wife.
<v Carmen Delzell>我听说我们惩罚自己的方式比我们能想象到的任何灵魂或神灵都要更好、更深刻。我希望这是真的。我希望这是真的，因为我仍然感到内疚。因为，你看，大卫，那个百万富翁，是有妇之夫，我从一开始就知道。他邀请我去华盛顿特区和他妻子一起吃晚饭。

00:44:25.090 --> 00:45:06.470
<v Carmen Delzell>I remember everything about that night. I wore an antique dress, platform shoes, and three carved Bakelite bracelets. My entire outfit cost less than $10. His wife had a mink coat, and long, red fingernails. I was very impressed with them both. They'd invited me to drive from my little house in Virginia to discuss a business deal. He was a slum landlord. She had an antique clothing store. They had discovered me selling things at the Georgetown flea market and wanted me to trade clothes for a three-room apartment in a tenement across town. It seemed like a good idea to me at the time.
<v Carmen Delzell>我记得那晚的一切。我穿了一件古董裙，厚底鞋，戴着三个雕刻的电木手镯。我整套行头不到10美元。他妻子穿着貂皮大衣，留着长长的红指甲。他们俩都让我印象深刻。他们邀请我从我在弗吉尼亚的小屋开车去讨论一笔生意。他是贫民窟房东。她有一家古董服装店。他们在乔治敦跳蚤市场发现了我，想让我用衣服换取城另一边公寓楼里的一套三居室。当时我觉得这是个好主意。

00:45:06.470 --> 00:45:28.290
<v Carmen Delzell>So I took the place. But things didn't really go as they were intended to. The apartment was cramped and lonely. All the other tenants were on strike because the building was falling down. And David, the owner, wanted me to be his mistress. During this time, my visits to the local Haitian voodoo store became more frequent.
<v Carmen Delzell>所以我接下了那个地方。但事情并没有按预想的那样发展。公寓拥挤且孤独。其他房客都在罢工，因为那栋楼快塌了。而房东大卫想让我当他的情妇。这段时间，我去当地的海地巫毒店的次数变多了。

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:52.890
<v Carmen Delzell>You must understand that living in the ghetto was very different from living in any other part of town. Even the grocery stores sell statues of Santa Barbara, San Lazaro, and the Virgin Mary. And when one of the neighbors was found dead on the front stoop, the Jamaican woman whose son dressed like a ninja and practiced swinging his nunchucks in the hall put holy candles all around the lobby in the order of the seven African powers.
<v Carmen Delzell>你必须明白，住在贫民区和住在城里任何其他地方非常不同。甚至连杂货店里都卖圣芭芭拉、圣拉扎罗和圣母玛利亚的雕像。当其中一个邻居被发现死在前廊上时，那个儿子打扮得像忍者、在走廊里练习挥舞双节棍的牙买加女人，在门厅里点了一圈圣烛，摆成了“七大非洲力量”的阵型。

00:45:52.890 --> 00:46:10.330
<v Carmen Delzell>We were the only white family on the block. It was very lonely. My only comfort was sitting in the cool, spooky Catholic church looking at the handmade doll clothes the ladies made for the statues.
<v Carmen Delzell>我们是那个街区唯一的白人家庭。非常孤独。我唯一的安慰是坐在阴凉、阴森的天主教堂里，看着女士们为雕像亲手缝制的娃娃衣服。

00:46:10.330 --> 00:46:44.320
<v Carmen Delzell>And then, one full moon, I put on my black velvet dress pinned with a rhinestone dagger, wrapped a small box of jinx remover powder in a white handkerchief, and walked across town to the house where David and his wife lived. It was not really clear to me at the time what I was up to. I felt as if I was being guided by an unseen force, but I swear, I had no real idea what I was doing. I took a pine branch and I put it through the mail slot. And then I left this little package on the stoop, said a few magic words, and ran.
<v Carmen Delzell>然后，在一个满月之夜，我穿上那条别着水钻匕首的黑色天鹅绒裙子，用白手帕包上一小盒去晦粉，穿过城市走到大卫和他妻子住的那栋房子。当时我不太清楚自己在干什么。我觉得自己被一股看不见的力量引导着，但我发誓，我真的不知道自己在做什么。我拿了一根松树枝，把它插进邮箱缝里。然后我把这个小包裹留在门廊上，说了几句咒语，就跑了。

00:46:44.320 --> 00:47:08.400
<v Carmen Delzell>The next morning, when David came out to get the paper, he found the stick and the spell. His wife asked him to go buy half and half for their coffee because they were having weekend guests. David says he doesn't really know why he decided to go to the little tienda by my house, but when he got there, he decided to come see me. Until that moment, neither one of us had felt anything for the other.
<v Carmen Delzell>第二天早上，当大卫出来拿报纸时，发现了棍子和符咒。他妻子让他去买半牛奶用来冲咖啡，因为他们要有周末客人在家。大卫说他不知道为什么自己决定去我家旁边的那家小店，但当他走到那儿时，他决定来见我。在那一刻之前，我们俩对彼此都没有任何感觉。

00:47:08.400 --> 00:47:40.960
<v Carmen Delzell>When I opened the door, he was standing there crying. He said he loved me and couldn't live without me. I said the same thing, and he never went back home. His wife sued him for $1 million, all the furniture, and the Mercedes. She won, but I got him. It took me years to figure out how to get away.
<v Carmen Delzell>当我打开门时，他站在那儿哭了。他说他爱我，不能没有我。我也说了同样的话，他就再也没回过家。他妻子起诉他索赔100万美元，以及所有的家具和那辆梅赛德斯。她赢了，但我得到了他。我花了好几年才弄明白怎么逃离。

00:47:40.960 --> 00:48:03.270
<v Carmen Delzell>It's true that I loved him for a while, or at least I thought I did. But looking back on it, I realize I had been alone so long that I would have probably thought I was in love with anybody who took care of me. I remember when he bought me this Diana Ross song, "I Know I'll Never Love This Way Again." I'd play that before he came over and dance around the house and try to get in the mood.
<v Carmen Delzell>我确实爱过他一段时间，或者至少我是这么认为的。但回想起来，我意识到我孤独的时间太长了，可能任何照顾我的人我都会觉得我爱上了他。我记得当他给我买那首戴安娜·罗斯的歌《我知道我永远不会再这样去爱了》时。在找我之前，我常播放它，在屋子里跳舞，试图让自己进入状态。

00:48:03.270 --> 00:48:29.750
<v Carmen Delzell>Then my brother was found half-dead of a barbiturate overdose in the woods of West Virginia on a commune, and he came to live with us. Four people in a three-room apartment. One thing led to another, and David took us all under his wing. Michael tried to kill himself two more times. Christmas came and went. David gave me $40 a week and took me out to dinner, which sometimes cost three times that.
<v Carmen Delzell>然后我弟弟被发现因为巴比妥酸盐过量而半死不活地躺在西弗吉尼亚森林里的一个公社里，他搬来和我们一起住。三居室公寓里住了四个人。事情一件接一件，大卫把我们所有人都照顾起来。迈克尔又尝试自杀了两次。圣诞节来了又去了。大卫每周给我40美元，带我出去吃饭，有时候饭钱是那个的三倍。

00:48:29.750 --> 00:49:04.790
<v Carmen Delzell>I felt like I was El Salvador and David was America. I used to scream and cry and beg him to let me go. One of the neighbors put it into words very well when he carved "landlord's whore" on my front door. I refused to let David have it painted over. It was true. There are only two kinds of frenzy that take all my reason away. One is the frenzy of love, one is the frenzy of needing money. It got to the point where I was in a constant frenzy. I needed a lawyer and a psychiatrist.
<v Carmen Delzell>我觉得自己像萨尔瓦多，大卫像美国。我常尖叫、哭泣并求他放我走。其中一个邻居在我的前门上刻下“房东的婊子”时，很好地用词表达了出来。我拒绝让大卫把那字涂掉。那是真的。只有两种狂乱会让我丧失理智。一种是爱的狂乱，一种是需要钱的狂乱。我陷入了不断的狂乱中。我需要律师和精神科医生。

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:35.060
<v Carmen Delzell>My cripple brother moved across the street to another hot slum apartment and lived with a black woman named May. They fought over money, other men, his leaving his clothes on the floor for her to pick up, et cetera. She was very beautiful, like Diana Ross, but she stayed drunk on straight gin and watched soap operas all day. David gave him $200 a week to live on. It was part of our understanding even though we never discussed it.
<v Carmen Delzell>我那个瘸腿弟弟搬到街对面另一间炎热的贫民窟公寓，和一个叫梅的黑人女人住在一起。他们为钱、为别的男人、为他把衣服丢在地板上让她收拾之类的事而吵架。她非常美，像戴安娜·罗斯，但她整天沉溺在纯杜松子酒中，看着肥皂剧。大卫每周给他200美元维持生活。这是我们默契的一部分，尽管我们从未讨论过。

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:56.480
<v Carmen Delzell>I knew that my staying with him kept my brother from another suicide attempt. The money gave us all a false sense of security, but the thought of it being taken away haunted us constantly. I was 30 years old. The children were eight and four. My brother was 24. And David was 33 and already a millionaire.
<v Carmen Delzell>我知道我留在他身边阻止了我弟弟另一次自杀企图。这笔钱给了我们所有人虚假的安稳感，但它随时会被拿走的念头一直萦绕着我们。我30岁。孩子们8岁和4岁。我弟弟24岁。大卫33岁，已经是个百万富翁了。

00:49:56.480 --> 00:50:12.550
<v Carmen Delzell>Then one hot night in June, I began to break all the windows in the apartment with my bare hands. I began to scream out the window that this is what happened to a woman when she had to sell her body to pay the rent. David tried to hold me down and cover my mouth, but I broke away and ran down three flights of stairs screaming.
<v Carmen Delzell>然后在一个炎热的六月晚上，我开始用裸手打破公寓里的所有窗户。我开始对着窗外尖叫，这就是一个女人为了付房租不得不出卖身体的下场。大卫试图按住我、捂住我的嘴，但我挣脱了，跑下三层楼梯，一路尖叫。

00:50:12.550 --> 00:50:34.080
<v Carmen Delzell>When I got out on the cement steps, he and his brother caught me again. But before they realized what was happening, I ripped my dress in two and stood there naked and yelling, "Take it, take it. This is all you want. This is what you pay for, my body, not me, just this body." His brother slapped me in the face and they drug me naked up the cement stairs, scraping the skin off my back.
<v Carmen Delzell>当我冲到水泥台阶上时，他和他的兄弟又抓住了我。但在他们意识到发生了什么之前，我撕碎了衣服，赤身裸体站在那儿大喊：“拿去，拿去。这就是你们想要的。这就是你们花钱买的，我的身体，不是我，只是这具身体。”他兄弟扇了我一耳光，他们赤身裸体地把我拖上水泥楼梯，背上的皮都被蹭掉了。

00:50:34.080 --> 00:51:00.860
<v Carmen Delzell>I don't remember too much after that, but nothing really changed. We went on living there. My brother went on living across the street. Two years later, he finally did kill himself by jumping out the window. I kept his remains in the kitchen cabinet for a long time.
<v Carmen Delzell>之后的事我不记得太多了，但一切并没有改变。我们继续住在那儿。我弟弟继续住在那条街对面。两年后，他最终还是跳窗自杀了。我把他的骨灰在厨房柜子里放了很长时间。

00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:28.100
<v Carmen Delzell>But it took me seven years to get rid of David. Ronald Reagan was up for a second term that year, and he was invited to the inaugural ball. David wanted me to go, too. Now, I do know people who would be delighted to go to the inaugural ball just to see what it's like. But I was so superstitious, I was sure God would strike me dead for hobnobbing with the richest and meanest congregation on Earth.
<v Carmen Delzell>但我花了七年时间才甩掉大卫。那年罗纳德·里根竞选第二个任期，他被邀请参加就职舞会。大卫也想让我去。现在，我确实认识有些人会很高兴去参加就职舞会，看看那是什么样子的。但我太迷信了，我肯定上帝会因为我和地球上最富有、最刻薄的信徒聚在一起而把我打死。

00:51:28.100 --> 00:51:58.230
<v Carmen Delzell>So I went back to the voodoo lady. She told me what to do. First I had to take an apple and cut out its core. Then I was to write my problem on a piece of paper and put it inside. Then I had to wrap the apple with a black ribbon while I chanted, bind him, bind him, take away his power and set me free. And then, on the first night of the new moon, I buried the apple in the nuns' graveyard behind the Sacred Heart Church.
<v Carmen Delzell>所以我又找了那个巫毒女士。她告诉了我该怎么做。首先我得拿个苹果，挖掉它的核。然后我要把我的问题写在纸条上塞进去。然后我要用黑色丝带缠住苹果，一边念咒，绑定他，绑定他，夺走他的力量，让我自由。然后，在新月的第一个晚上，我把苹果埋在圣心教堂后面的修女墓地里。

00:51:58.230 --> 00:52:16.840
<v Carmen Delzell>The next day, the children and I drove to Cincinnati where an old Hell's Angel friend of mine ran a tattoo parlor. I got him to put a big red heart and dagger up high on my bicep. I knew no good Republican man would ever want to be seen with me again. And you know what? He didn't.
<v Carmen Delzell>第二天，孩子们和我开车去了辛辛那提，我一个老“地狱天使”朋友在那儿经营一家纹身店。我让他把一颗大红心和匕首纹在我的肱二头肌高处。我知道不会再有任何一个体面的共和党人想再见到我。你知道吗？他真的没再想了。

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:41.350
<v Carmen Delzell>The truth is that like any other Third World country that tries to cut itself off from America too quickly, our economy has never recovered. And that's why tonight by the full moon, I have returned to the bar where we first made our fateful bargain to beg him for enough money to pay the rent. They say that once you start messing with the occult, you're forever in its debt.
<v Carmen Delzell>真相是，像任何试图太快切断与美国联系的第三世界国家一样，我们的经济从未复苏。这就是为什么今晚满月时，我又回到了我们最初做出致命交易的那个酒吧，求他给我足够的钱付房租。人们说，一旦你开始接触神秘学，你将永远负债。

00:52:41.350 --> 00:53:26.960
<v Ira Glass>Carmen Delzell lives in Buda, Texas. She just opened a vintage clothing store called Lucky Day.
<v Ira Glass>卡门·德尔泽尔住在德克萨斯州的布达。她刚刚开了一家名为“幸运日”的复古服装店。

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:53.630
<v Ira Glass>Act Five, First Contact. You know, there's been so many retellings of the Faust story-- Marlowe, Goethe, Sayers, Thomas Mann, there are various versions of the opera Faust-- but only one person, as far as we can determine, only one person has attempted to tell the story of the devil coming to Earth to steal someone's soul as a children's story. That person is Daniel Pinkwater, and he has agreed to read his book Devil in the Drain, to end our program today.
<v Ira Glass>第五幕，第一次接触。你知道，关于浮士德故事的重述有很多——马洛、歌德、塞耶斯、托马斯·曼，还有各种版本的歌剧《浮士德》——但据我们所知，只有一个人，只有一个人试图以儿童故事的形式讲述恶魔来到地球偷走某人灵魂的故事。那个人就是丹尼尔·平克沃特，他同意朗读他的书《排水沟里的魔鬼》，以此结束我们今天的节目。

00:53:53.630 --> 00:54:09.920
<v Daniel Pinkwater>I knew that the devil lived inside our plumbing. I could hear him making noises, especially down the drain of the kitchen sink. Sometimes, I would look into the drainpipe with a flashlight and try to see him. Finally, I caught a glimpse of something down there.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>我知道恶魔住在我们的管道里。我能听到他发出声音，特别是在厨房水槽的排水沟里。有时候，我会用手电筒往排水管里看，试图看到他。最终，我瞥见了下面有什么东西。

00:54:09.920 --> 00:54:12.330
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Hey," I said, "are you the devil?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“嘿，”我说，“你是恶魔吗？”

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:15.980
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"What if I am?" A voice from down the drain answered.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“那又怎样？”排水沟下传来一个声音。

00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:18.950
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I just want to know, that's all," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我只是想知道，仅此而已，”我说。

00:54:18.950 --> 00:54:22.380
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I don't have to tell you anything," the voice said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我没必要告诉你任何事，”那个声音说。

00:54:22.380 --> 00:54:24.690
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Come up here so I can see you," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“上来，让我看看你，”我说。

00:54:24.690 --> 00:54:29.220
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Oh no, you'll turn on the water as soon as I get there, and get me all wet."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“哦不，你一见我就会打开水龙头，把我淋得浑身湿透。”

00:54:29.220 --> 00:54:31.420
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"No I won't. Honestly."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“不，我不会。真的。”

00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:33.810
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Well, that's what I would do."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“嗯，换做是我也会这么做。”

00:54:33.810 --> 00:54:35.590
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"You are the devil, aren't you?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你是恶魔，对吧？”

00:54:35.590 --> 00:54:37.940
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Yes."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“是的。”

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:45.050
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'd really like to have a look at you. If I absolutely promise not to turn on the water, will you come up so I can see you?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我真的很想看看你。如果我绝对保证不开水，你上来让我看看行吗？”

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:47.110
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Why do you want to see me?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“为什么你想见我？”

00:54:47.110 --> 00:54:55.020
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I just do. Come on up. I won't hurt you. Besides, there are those little bar things across the drain. I couldn't get at you if I wanted to."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我就是想。上来吧。我不会伤害你的。再说，排水口上有那些细栅栏。就算我想干什么也够不着你。”

00:54:55.020 --> 00:54:58.960
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'm not afraid of you. I'm the devil."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我不怕你。我是恶魔。”

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:00.920
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Then come up and let me see."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“那就上来让我看看。”

00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:05.660
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"All right, but if you turn on the water, I'll do some pretty bad things to you."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“好吧，但如果你打开水，我会让你吃不了兜着走。”

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:07.540
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"You're really suspicious," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你真的很疑神疑鬼，”我说。

00:55:07.540 --> 00:55:13.000
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"You would be too if you were me," the devil said. His voice sounded closer.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“如果我是你，你也会这样，”恶魔说。他的声音听起来更近了。

00:55:13.000 --> 00:55:14.930
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Are you coming up?" I asked down the drain.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你要上来了吗？”我冲排水沟下面问。

00:55:14.930 --> 00:55:20.290
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Yes, I'm coming," the devil said. "And don't say it's my fault if you get scared to death."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“是的，我要上来了，”恶魔说。“如果你被吓个半死，别怪我。”

00:55:20.290 --> 00:55:21.740
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'm not scared," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我不怕，”我说。

00:55:21.740 --> 00:55:24.210
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Well, you should be."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“嗯，你应该怕的。”

00:55:24.210 --> 00:55:41.350
<v Daniel Pinkwater>I could see the devil climbing up the drain. He was pretty small. He wasn't any bigger than the goldfish I had lost down that drain once when I was changing the water in the fishbowl. The devil got up to the little crossed bars. He had a face that reminded me of the fish, and he was almost the same color.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>我可以看到恶魔正在顺着排水管爬上来。他挺小的。还没我以前换鱼缸水时掉进下水道的那条金鱼大。恶魔爬到了小十字栅栏那儿。他有一张让我想起那条鱼的脸，颜色也几乎一模一样。

00:55:41.350 --> 00:55:46.410
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Well, here I am," the devil said. "You're terrified, right?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“好了，我到了，”恶魔说。“你吓坏了吧，对吧？”

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:49.830
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Do you remember when I lost my fish down this drain?" I asked.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你还记得我那条鱼掉进这个下水道了吗？”我问。

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:52.580
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Sure I do," the devil said. "You murdered him."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“当然记得，”恶魔说。“你谋杀了它。”

00:55:52.580 --> 00:55:54.620
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I did not. It was an accident."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我没有。那是场意外。”

00:55:54.620 --> 00:55:58.610
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Sure it was," the devil said. "Poor fish, he probably trusted you."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“当然是，”恶魔说。“可怜的鱼，它可能还信任你呢。”

00:55:58.610 --> 00:56:06.310
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"The water overflowed, and he got sloshed out of the bowl and down the drain," I said. "I don't think he was smart enough to trust me or not trust me. Fish are dumb."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“水溢出来了，它被冲出了鱼缸，掉进了下水道，”我说。“我不觉得它聪明到会信任我或不信任我。鱼是很笨的。”

00:56:06.310 --> 00:56:12.210
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"That's right," the devil said. "Tell yourself that. I heard him crying for help. You didn't even care."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“说得好，”恶魔说。“你就这么对自己说吧。我听见它在求救。你根本不在乎。”

00:56:12.210 --> 00:56:14.800
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I did care," I said. "But I didn't kill him on purpose."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我在乎，”我说。“但我不是故意杀它的。”

00:56:14.800 --> 00:56:18.190
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"But you admit you killed him."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“但你承认是你杀了它。”

00:56:18.190 --> 00:56:19.620
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Is that what you do?" I asked.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“这就是你做的事吗？”我问。

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:20.510
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"What?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“什么？”

00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:23.060
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Try to make people feel bad about things they can't help?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“试图让别人对自己无法控制的事情感到内疚？”

00:56:23.060 --> 00:56:29.020
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"It's one of the things I do," the devil said. "Now admit it, you feel pretty horrible about killing that fish, right?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“这是我做的事情之一，”恶魔说。“现在承认吧，你对自己害死那条鱼感到很糟糕，对吧？”

00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:36.400
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I didn't kill him," I said, "and to tell the truth, I don't feel that bad about it. I'm sorry it happened, but it wasn't my fault."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我没杀它，”我说，“而且说实话，我没觉得有多糟糕。很遗憾发生这种事，但不是我的错。”

00:56:36.400 --> 00:56:44.630
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'm getting bored talking about your stupid fish," the devil said. "How come you haven't mentioned how frightening I am, and at the same time, sort of fascinating?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“谈论你那条笨鱼真让人无聊，”恶魔说。“你怎么没提我有多吓人，同时又有点迷人？”

00:56:44.630 --> 00:56:47.800
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"You look a little like a fish," I said. "How come you live in the plumbing?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你看起来有点像条鱼，”我说。“你怎么会住在管道里？”

00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:53.280
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Again with the fish," the devil said. "I live in the plumbing because I can do whatever I please."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“又是鱼，”恶魔说。“我住在管道里是因为我想干嘛就干嘛。”

00:56:53.280 --> 00:56:54.580
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"How come you're so small?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“你怎么这么小？”

00:56:54.580 --> 00:57:02.600
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'm as big as I need to be," the devil said. "Now how about you getting me a pretzel? Do you have any of those skinny salty ones?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我需要多大就能有多大，”恶魔说。“给我拿个椒盐卷饼怎么样？你有那种细细咸咸的吗？”

00:57:02.600 --> 00:57:07.040
<v Daniel Pinkwater>I got the devil a pretzel. I pushed it down the drain. He ate it.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>我给恶魔拿了个椒盐卷饼。我把它塞进下水道。他吃了。

00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:12.040
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"That was lousy," he said. "I know you only got it for me because you're so frightened of me."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“太难吃了，”他说。“我知道你给我只是因为你怕我。”

00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:22.620
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Look," I said, "I got you the pretzel because you asked for it. You want another pretzel, I'll get you another pretzel. I happen to be a good-natured kid, and I'm not in the least afraid of you."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“听着，”我说，“我给你椒盐卷饼是因为你要。你还想要，我再给你拿一个。我正好是个好脾气的孩子，一点也不怕你。”

00:57:22.620 --> 00:57:24.310
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Yes I do," the devil said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我还想要，”恶魔说。

00:57:24.310 --> 00:57:24.780
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Do what?"
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“干什么？”

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:26.450
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Yes, I do want another pretzel."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“没错，我还要一个椒盐卷饼。”

00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:28.130
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'll get it."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我去拿。”

00:57:28.130 --> 00:57:32.960
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Ha, the kid's terrified," the devil said to himself. I heard him say it.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“哈，这孩子吓坏了，”恶魔自言自语道。我听到了。

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:37.650
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"That does it," I said. I turned on both taps, hot and cold.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“够了，”我说。我打开了冷热水龙头。

00:57:37.650 --> 00:57:40.690
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Hey, no fair," the devil shouted.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“嘿，不公平，”恶魔大喊。

00:57:40.690 --> 00:57:44.010
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"Too bad," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“真遗憾，”我说。

00:57:44.010 --> 00:57:49.520
<v Daniel Pinkwater>I heard the devil shouting and gurgling a long way down the drain pipe. He was really mad.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>我听见恶魔在排水管深处尖叫和打水花。他气疯了。

00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:53.760
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"That was a rotten trick," he shouted. "You ought to be ashamed."
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“那是卑鄙的诡计，”他大叫。“你应该感到羞耻。”

00:57:53.760 --> 00:57:58.190
<v Daniel Pinkwater>"I'm not," I said.
<v Daniel Pinkwater>“我没有，”我说。

00:57:58.190 --> 00:58:20.310
<v Ira Glass>Daniel Pinkwater's book, Devil in the Drain, has been banned by children's libraries all over America. Really.
<v Ira Glass>丹尼尔·平克沃特的书《排水沟里的魔鬼》已被全美各地的儿童图书馆禁阅。真的。

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:26.530
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced today by Nancy Updike, Alix Spiegel, Peter Clowney, and myself. Contributing editors, Paul Tough, Jack Hit, and Margy Rochlin.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们今天的节目由南希·厄普代克、阿丽克斯·斯皮格尔、彼得·克劳尼和我制作。特约编辑：保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特和玛吉·罗克林。

00:58:26.530 --> 00:58:29.810
